## TUI terminal ownership, configurable hotkeys, and Groq rate controls — 2026-11-15

- Added a pausable terminal input reader that acknowledges pauses before child-process launch and reads at most one byte per call, preventing background input consumption and buffered read-ahead into the editor or shell.
- External editors and shells receive foreground process-group ownership. Agy restores its foreground group, raw mode, bracketed paste, enhanced keyboard, and mouse policy on return.
- Model streaming output and UI events are buffered while a child owns the terminal, then flushed/replayed after return. Ctrl+O opens an interactive `$SHELL` even while an LLM request runs; the request context is not cancelled. Ctrl+G remains the prompt editor. Alt+O toggles tool trajectory.
- Ctrl+M is normalized to Enter in enhanced keyboard protocols. Ctrl+J remains newline. IEXTEN is disabled in raw mode so Ctrl+W is handled by Agy rather than the terminal line discipline.
- The model picker Tab key applies the highlighted model in both scrollback and full-screen modes.
- settings.json exposes validated action-to-key mappings; /keybindings displays active assignments. Conflicting bindings are rejected and empty values unbind actions.
- Groq has shared RPM/TPM limiting, configurable output-token cap and 429 retries, and Retry-After/reset-hint parsing. Groq tool requests explicitly use `tool_choice: auto`; GPT-OSS disables parallel tool calls.
- Verified Groq's local function-call protocol with request-body tests: function definitions are sent with `tool_choice: auto`, assistant function calls are followed by tool-role messages carrying the matching `tool_call_id`, and GPT-OSS requests disable parallel calls.
- Added a mocked Groq 429 test using `X-RateLimit-Reset-Tokens`; Agy waits for the reset window and succeeds on the next request. Retry-delay tests cover Retry-After, token-reset duration, and the human-readable ITPM retry phrase.
- Added hotkey defaults, Ctrl+M alias, collision detection, remap/unbind and JSON round-trip tests. Input-reader tests verify child-shell keystrokes are not consumed while Agy's reader is paused.
- Work CI run #1917 (`43679378944`) passed Bash checks, gofmt, `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`, Linux amd64 build and artifact upload.
- Remaining: manually verify terminal handoff in an interactive xterm/terminal emulator and adjust any emulator-specific foreground-process behavior reported by real use. No release or main merge requested.

- Tool availability is role- and permission-scoped before the request reaches Groq. Built-in researcher/reviewer/planner roles are read-only and can receive file listing/read/search and web tools, but not shell/write/edit tools. The no-tools role intentionally receives no function tools. Use the default/coder role and an appropriate global permission mode for workspace operations. A claim that only web search is available should be checked against the active `/agent` role and `/config` permissions; a subsequent ITPM 429 is a separate quota failure.


## Provider switching blocked by default reasoning effort — 2026-11-15

- Diagnosed the released v0.8.6 provider-switch failure: NVIDIA API models initialize with the provider-native reasoning effort `default`, but `Agent.UpdateConfig()` accepted only `low`, `medium`, `high`, and `xhigh`. The TUI applies `modelProvider` through `UpdateConfig()`, so every provider change was rejected before the new client was installed. The same validation could reject unrelated Configuration saves while a provider-default effort was active.
- Added shared `config.IsSupportedEffort()` validation for `default`, `none`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`. Both `config.SetSetting()` and `Agent.UpdateConfig()` now use it; values are normalized to lowercase.
- Added tests ensuring the Configuration effort editor accepts every advertised value and rejects unsupported values. Added an agent regression test that starts on NVIDIA with `Effort=default`, switches to OpenAI-compatible, and verifies that the provider and native default effort are preserved.
- Implementation is on `work`; the published v0.8.6 release and `main` are unchanged. A new build/release is required to deliver the fix to the released binary.
- CI verification is pending for the latest `work` commit. Do not merge or publish without an explicit request.


## Full TUI completion, navigation, configuration and Markdown renderer — 2026-11-15

- Added cursor-aware slash-command and argument completion. Replacements operate on the token at the cursor and preserve text on both sides. Commands use prefix matches first, then fuzzy ranking with adjacent-key typo weighting. A partial slash command such as `/com` now executes the best-ranked command on the first Enter in both full-screen and scrollback modes; Tab remains available to accept or cycle suggestions explicitly.
- Expanded argument suggestions for roles, system-prompt profiles, models, supported effort levels, permissions, statusline/theme/sandbox options, plugin actions and existing plugin names, session/recovery IDs, workspace paths, and common valid `/max-turns` values. Removed misleading provider-argument completions from `/login` and `/logout`, which act on the active provider and do not accept those arguments.
- Unified Vim-style selection behavior across overlays: `j/k` and arrows move the selection, `g/G` jump to the first/last row, Page Up/Down scroll by a page, and `/` enters fuzzy search. Search keeps option values and setting keys attached to their displayed rows; Enter finishes search, Escape clears it, and an empty result cannot be selected. The model picker now reserves `j/k` for navigation and uses `/` to enter filter mode.
- Replaced the flat Configuration list with nested groups for provider/credentials, model/reasoning, NVIDIA and Groq rate limits, provider-specific model filters, session/execution, permissions/security, Gemini safety filters, interface/appearance, editor, keyboard shortcuts and system prompt. Escape returns one menu level at a time. Added an invariant test that every setting is represented exactly once.
- Added a terminal Markdown renderer based on Aichat's stateful fence/language and streaming-rendering approach (reference pinned at `aichat-reference`). Full-screen assistant transcript rendering now supports headings, paragraphs, emphasis, inline code/links, blockquotes, nested ordered/unordered/task lists, horizontal rules, tables, fenced code, language-aware syntax colors and terminal-cell wrapping. Scrollback output renders incrementally while buffering incomplete lines and retaining fenced-code language state. The implementation uses a lightweight Go renderer; it does not link the Rust Aichat/Syntect runtime.
- Corrected fullscreen overlay footer duplication, handled fenced-code info-string metadata, wrapped by terminal display cells (including wide Unicode and tabs), and added tests for renderer output, table width, streamed partial lines and menu presentation.
- Provider switching regression: released v0.8.6 could reject all Configuration saves while NVIDIA's provider-native effort was `default`. Shared effort validation now accepts every value exposed by the configuration editor. A regression test switches from NVIDIA with `Effort=default` to the OpenAI-compatible provider.
- Verification: GitHub Actions CI runs #1988, #1989, #1990, #1991 and #1992 completed successfully. The latest run #1992 (`43700645383`) passed Bash completion checks, shell syntax checks, gofmt, `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and the Linux amd64 build/artifact step.
- Work remains isolated on branch `work`. No merge to `main`, tag or release was created. The v0.8.6 binary remains unchanged; the provider-switch fix and TUI improvements require a subsequent build/release to reach installed binaries.
- Runtime caveat: CI covers code paths and terminal rendering logic, but the interactive foreground-process/terminal behavior has not been manually exercised in a live xterm session. No live emulator verification is claimed.


